  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Friday)\nIssued at 1024 PM CDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nThe Mid-South will begin a warming trend after an overall\npleasant day with dewpoints in the 50s and 60s and highs in the\nupper 80s to lower 90s. Upper-level ridging, currently centered\nover the Four Corners Region, will begin broadening east Saturday\nwith highs spanning the 90s Saturday and the entire area seeing\nreadings in the mid to upper 90s by Sunday. Luckily, dry air from\nthe Midwest will persist over the region keeping dewpoints in the\n60s to lower 70s. This will create a hot and dry feel, as opposed\nto a hot and humid one, with heat indices in the lower 100s\nthrough early next week. LREF guidance continues to paint low\nprobabilities (<15%) for heat indices 105+F both Monday and\nTuesday, likely keeping us away from Heat Advisory criteria\nthrough early week.\n\nMoving into midweek will likely be a different story as the\naforementioned upper ridge continues to broaden over the\nMississippi Valley. GFS and EWCF solutions continue to hint at a\nvery weak upper-low beginning to retrograde across the\nMississippi Valley as Gulf moisture begins to infiltrate.\nDewpoints look to begin increasing into the low to mid 70s, in\nturn, allowing us to begin flirting with 105+F heat indices. We\nwill evaluate heat headlines on a day-to-day basis, as heat index\nvalues will depend significantly on dewpoint temperatures through\nthe end of the workweek. Isolated (20%) chances for precipitation\nenter the forecast Wednesday into early Thursday with increased\nmoisture. Though any precipitation that does fall will not be\nappreciable and will only result in increased humidity across the\nMid-South. Otherwise, we'll remain largely dry over the next\nseven days. Looking ahead, the latest 8-14 day outlook has us\nhighlighted with a 70-80% chance of above-normal temperatures\nthrough the first week and a half of September.\n\nAEH",
